  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating which lottery concentrates more probability on favorable (less negative) outcomes—i.e., which is more left-skewed. This would involve comparing the 80% chance of 0 in A versus 75% in B, leading to a preference for A. However, the individual prefers B and justifies this based on the smaller worst-case loss (-3000 > -4000), not on the probability distribution around favorable outcomes. While probabilities are mentioned, they are used to downplay the difference in likelihoods, not to assess skewness. The individual’s focus is on outcome severity and risk minimization, not on the asymmetry of the probability distribution. There is no evidence that the individual assessed the skewness of the lotteries or used it as a decision rule. The reasoning is inconsistent with the skewness-based reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
